A||||1||VISCERAL||Aligning a door?
Z||000000||VISCERAL||07-06-99||10:57 AM||||Hey, my diver's door is too close to the front fender and rubs. I've replaced the bushings and pins to no avail. Do I have to take off the inner door panel and the outer door skin to gain access to the bracket bolts in the door and back out the door bracket bolts to lift and pull the door towards the rear?<br>BTW: car has not been in an accident, it's just an old 87 GT. <p>Thanks, Patrick Szewczyk
Z||000001||theogre||07-06-99||03:27 PM||||There are 2 adjustment planes for the door hinges. You may have to work both adjustments to get the door correct. You will likely need 2-3 people, and some wood/plastic wedges, to do this job. The wedges help hold fine adjustment. the door is heavy and hard to keep still.<p>For front/rear adjustment you have to remove some under dash trim and slide the hinges back. For in/out you have to move the door on the hinges which will require getting inside the door. <p>This assumes the fender is in correct alignment at all it's mounting points. If it's not, then adjusting the door likely won't be posible. there is very little extra clearance.
